# Device and account management

A registered device is a remote control target owned by your Pontia Cloud user. Its registration, local encryption identity, selected Edge, and local service must all agree before remote access works.

## Check a device

Run on the registered device:

```sh
pontia doctor
```

The command checks the local device ID, Cloud registration, selected Edge, and stored end-to-end encryption identity. It requires a valid CLI credential from `pontia login`.

## Rename a device or change its handle

Use [Device settings](/settings/devices), or run:

```sh
pontia remote edit --name "Build workstation"
pontia remote edit --handle build-workstation
```

Both options may be supplied together. Changing the handle updates the device URL at `app.pontia.dev/HANDLE` while preserving its encryption identity and selected Edge.

<h2 id="switch-edge">Switch Edge</h2>

Run `pontia remote edit` without options. Press Enter to keep the current name and handle, select an Edge by name, and confirm. Each prompt defaults to the current value; keeping all three values exits without restarting the service. `--name` and `--handle` continue to change only those fields without prompting.

Switching Edge keeps the device identity and Dashboard URL. Your existing CLI login is reused. A running managed service restarts; a stopped service uses the new target on the next `pontia up`. Restart an unmanaged `pontiad` manually. Reconnect or refresh the Dashboard after switching.

The command reports whether the target was saved and the service restarted; this does not verify remote connectivity. If saving succeeds but restarting fails, fix the service and run `pontia up`, or restart an unmanaged service manually. If the save result is uncertain, run `pontia remote edit` to inspect the saved values before applying them.

## Rotate the device key

Run:

```sh
pontia remote rotate-key
```

Pontia creates a new device E2E identity, updates the Cloud registration, writes the replacement to `$PONTIA_HOME/e2e-identity.json`, and restarts the local service. Existing browser/device encrypted sessions no longer match the new identity and must reconnect.

Use this command when the identity file may have been exposed or you want to replace the device's cryptographic identity.

## Disable remote access

On the device:

```sh
pontia remote disable
```

This unregisters the device, removes its local E2E identity, updates local configuration, and restarts the service. The CLI credential is preserved for subsequent Cloud commands.

Removing a device in [Device settings](/settings/devices) unregisters that device. Your CLI login and other registered devices remain available.

## Manage sign-in methods

In [Account settings](/settings/account), link Google or GitHub as sign-in methods. Each provider account can be linked to only one Pontia user. Pontia identifies provider accounts by their stable provider identity rather than treating matching email addresses as the same account.

Keep at least one working sign-in method linked to your user.

## Manage browser sessions

In [Session settings](/settings/sessions), you can revoke one browser session or sign out every other browser. A revoked browser session cannot refresh after its current access token expires.
